From: Kapy CAD <dev@kapy.dev>
Subject: [PATCH] BRepGProp_Gauss: devirtualise the finite-domain Gauss integrand

Under emscripten, every indirect call is wrapped in an `invoke_*` trampoline
that leaves WebAssembly, runs a JS shim and re-enters. BRepGProp_Gauss calls
its `add` / `mult` integrand through function pointers, once per Gauss point:
a CPU profile of BRepGProp::SurfaceProperties showed 58% of the time in
invoke_ddd + wasm-to-js + js-to-wasm, and only 18% in the integration itself.

The pointers only ever take two values, and the second (AddInf / MultInf) is
selected in checkBounds() for infinite domains. This adds a fast path that
calls ::Add / ::Mult directly when the domain is finite; the body is the
original one, verbatim, with the two calls devirtualised. Clang then inlines
them and the trampolines disappear.

Measured on OCCT 8.0 / emsdk 3.1.74, per face of BRepGProp::SurfaceProperties:
  planar      335 us -> 119 us
  cylindrical 921 us -> 253 us
which restores parity with the opencascade.js 7.7 build (100 / 245 us).
Native builds are unaffected: an indirect call there is just a call.

diff --git a/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKTopAlgo/BRepGProp/BRepGProp_Gauss.cxx b/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKTopAlgo/BRepGProp/BRepGProp_Gauss.cxx
index 853f746f..4e708669 100644
--- a/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKTopAlgo/BRepGProp/BRepGProp_Gauss.cxx
+++ b/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKTopAlgo/BRepGProp/BRepGProp_Gauss.cxx
@@ -385,6 +385,42 @@ void BRepGProp_Gauss::computeSInertiaOfElementaryPart(const gp_Pnt&
                                                       const double              theWeight,
                                                       BRepGProp_Gauss::Inertia& theOutInertia)
 {
+  // KAPY PATCH: on the finite path, call ::Add / ::Mult directly instead of
+  // through the `add` / `mult` function pointers. Emscripten wraps every
+  // indirect call in an `invoke_ddd` trampoline (wasm -> JS -> wasm), and this
+  // routine runs once per Gauss point: those trampolines were 58% of
+  // SurfaceProperties. The body below is the original, verbatim, with the two
+  // calls devirtualised; the infinite-domain path keeps the pointers.
+  if (add == static_cast<BRepGProp_GaussFunc>(&::Add))
+  {
+    // ds - Jacobien (x, y, z) -> (u, v) = ||n||
+    const double ds = ::Mult(theNormal.Magnitude(), theWeight);
+    const double x  = ::Add(thePoint.X(), -theLocation.X());
+    const double y  = ::Add(thePoint.Y(), -theLocation.Y());
+    const double z  = ::Add(thePoint.Z(), -theLocation.Z());
+
+    theOutInertia.Mass = ::Add(theOutInertia.Mass, ds);
+
+    const double XdS = ::Mult(x, ds);
+    const double YdS = ::Mult(y, ds);
+    const double ZdS = ::Mult(z, ds);
+
+    theOutInertia.Ix  = ::Add(theOutInertia.Ix, XdS);
+    theOutInertia.Iy  = ::Add(theOutInertia.Iy, YdS);
+    theOutInertia.Iz  = ::Add(theOutInertia.Iz, ZdS);
+    theOutInertia.Ixy = ::Add(theOutInertia.Ixy, ::Mult(x, YdS));
+    theOutInertia.Iyz = ::Add(theOutInertia.Iyz, ::Mult(y, ZdS));
+    theOutInertia.Ixz = ::Add(theOutInertia.Ixz, ::Mult(x, ZdS));
+
+    const double XXdS = ::Mult(x, XdS);
+    const double YYdS = ::Mult(y, YdS);
+    const double ZZdS = ::Mult(z, ZdS);
+
+    theOutInertia.Ixx = ::Add(theOutInertia.Ixx, ::Add(YYdS, ZZdS));
+    theOutInertia.Iyy = ::Add(theOutInertia.Iyy, ::Add(XXdS, ZZdS));
+    theOutInertia.Izz = ::Add(theOutInertia.Izz, ::Add(XXdS, YYdS));
+    return;
+  }
   // ds - Jacobien (x, y, z) -> (u, v) = ||n||
   const double ds = mult(theNormal.Magnitude(), theWeight);
   const double x  = add(thePoint.X(), -theLocation.X());
